menu "esp32-sofa-ble configuration"

    config SOFA_DEVICE_NAME_PREFIX
        string "Peripheral name prefix to connect to"
        default "Sofa_wwt"
        help
            A peripheral is only connected to when its advertised name starts
            with this. Seat count is encoded in the suffix: Sofa_wwt_01 ..
            Sofa_wwt_04.

            This mirrors the original app, which filters its scan by the 0xAE30
            service UUID and then requires /Sofa_wwt/ in the name. Matching on
            the service UUID *instead* of the name is tempting and wrong: other
            devices do advertise 0xAE30, and latching onto one of those means
            retrying a connection that never succeeds.

            Set this empty to match on the service UUID alone - useful if your
            sofa advertises a different name, or none. Any device advertising
            0xAE30 without a matching name is logged as a warning, so set this
            empty only after seeing that warning and meaning it.

    config SOFA_TARGET_ADDR
        string "Connect to this BLE address only (aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff)"
        default "ADDR_ANY"
        help
            Leave as ADDR_ANY to connect to the first matching peripheral found
            while scanning. Set a fixed address to pin the board to one sofa,
            which is worth doing once you know it - it skips the scan step and
            is the only way to talk to a sofa whose advertisement is filtered
            out by something else nearby.

            Use the 'sofa list' console command to discover it.

    config SOFA_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Connection attempt timeout (ms)"
        range 1000 120000
        default 30000

    config SOFA_SCAN_INTERVAL_MS
        int "BLE scan interval (ms)"
        range 20 10240
        default 100
        help
            How often the scanner listens, in milliseconds. Together with the
            window below this is the scan duty cycle.

            The controller default (a 10 ms window every 10 ms, a scan that
            never stops) takes airtime straight from WiFi on this single-radio
            chip, and buys nothing: measured here, 30 ms out of every 100 ms
            still found every advertiser in the room within about three
            seconds, including ones 90 dB down in the noise.

            Raise the interval (or lower the window) to give WiFi more room.
            Lower it only if the sofa is genuinely hard to find.

    config SOFA_SCAN_WINDOW_MS
        int "BLE scan window (ms)"
        range 10 10240
        default 30
        help
            How long each scan interval listens for. Must not exceed the
            interval; if it does it is clamped down to it.

            The default is 30 ms out of every 100 ms - a 30% duty cycle.

    config SOFA_RETRY_DELAY_MS
        int "Delay before rescanning after a disconnect or failed connect (ms)"
        range 0 60000
        default 3000

    config SOFA_HANDSHAKE
        bool "Send the 'fe5a' handshake after subscribing"
        default y
        help
            The original app writes fe5a to the write characteristic as soon as
            it has connected, discovered the characteristics and subscribed to
            notifications - before it enables any control. We do not know from
            static analysis whether the sofa *requires* it, so mirroring the app
            is the safe default. Turn this off to test whether it matters.

    config SOFA_JOG_REPEAT_MS
        int "Jog repeat interval (ms)"
        range 10 1000
        default 50
        help
            In jog ("点动") mode the sofa only moves while the button frame is
            repeated. The original app resends every 50 ms while the on-screen
            button is held.

    config SOFA_ANNOUNCE_NEW_DEVICES
        bool "Log every newly seen BLE device during a scan"
        default y
        help
            Useful when hunting for the sofa's address, noisy in normal use.

    config SOFA_STATUS_LED_GPIO
        int "Status LED GPIO"
        default 2
        help
            GPIO2 is this board's onboard LED. Steady on once *everything*
            works - the sofa link is ready and, if WiFi is configured, the
            station holds a lease. 1 Hz blink otherwise.

            Both links are folded into one light on purpose: from across the
            room the only question is "can I use this yet", and the answer is
            unambiguous. Which half is missing is in the log and in
            /api/status.

    config SOFA_STATUS_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
        bool "Status LED is active LOW"
        default n
        help
            Measured on this specific board: GPIO2 is active HIGH, which is the
            opposite of the usual classic-ESP32 wiring. See ../AGENTS.md.

    config SOFA_HOLD_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Stop a held seat if nothing checks in for this long (ms)"
        range 0 10000
        default 800
        help
            Only the HTTP control page uses this. In jog mode the *firmware* is
            what keeps re-sending the press frame, so if the phone loses WiFi,
            the browser tab is closed, or the page is backgrounded mid-press,
            the seat would keep moving forever. With the watchdog armed, the
            jog releases itself unless /api/hold keeps arriving (the dashboard
            sends it every 300 ms).

            The serial console never arms it, because a person sitting at a
            terminal is their own watchdog. Set this to 0 to disable it
            entirely - not recommended for the web page.

    # --- WiFi and the HTTP control surface ----------------------------------

    config SOFA_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID (2.4 GHz only)"
        default ""
        help
            SSID of the access point this board joins in station mode. Leave it
            empty and the board runs BLE-only: the sofa still works and the
            serial console still controls it, but there is no web page.

            The classic ESP32 radio is 2.4 GHz only - it cannot see a 5 GHz
            network. If your Mac is on 5 GHz and the board on 2.4 GHz they are
            usually still on the same LAN, but some routers isolate the bands,
            which is the first thing to check if the board gets an IP yet stays
            unreachable.

            Deliberately empty by default: credentials are never committed and
            never live in sdkconfig.defaults. Set this with

                idf.py menuconfig

            and it lands in sdkconfig, which .gitignore excludes.

    config SOFA_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi password"
        default ""
        help
            Passphrase for the SSID above. Leave empty for an open network; the
            station then uses the OPEN auth threshold regardless of the setting
            below.

    choice SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_THRESHOLD
        prompt "Weakest auth mode to accept when scanning"
        default SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_WPA2_PSK
        help
            The access point must advertise at least this strong an auth mode
            for the station to consider it. Only lower this if your AP uses
            legacy WPA (or WEP) and the board refuses to join.

        config SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_OPEN
            bool "OPEN"
        config SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_WPA_PSK
            bool "WPA PSK (also accepts WPA2/WPA3 APs)"
        config SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_WPA2_PSK
            bool "WPA2 PSK (also accepts WPA3 APs)"
        config SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_WPA3_PSK
            bool "WPA3 PSK"
        config SOFA_WIFI_AUTH_WPA2_WPA3_PSK
            bool "WPA2/WPA3 PSK"
    endchoice

    config SOFA_WIFI_RETRY_DELAY_MS
        int "Delay between WiFi reconnect attempts (ms)"
        default 2000
        range 100 60000
        help
            After a disconnect the station waits this long and then retries,
            forever. A control surface that gives up after five tries is not
            much of a control surface: the board has to come back on its own
            after a router reboot.

    config SOFA_HTTP_PORT
        int "HTTP server port"
        default 80
        range 1 65535
        help
            TCP port the control page and the JSON API listen on. Port 80 lets
            you browse to a bare http://<ip>/ with no port suffix.

    config SOFA_ENABLE_MDNS
        bool "Advertise the control page over mDNS (Bonjour)"
        default y
        help
            Publishes the board as <hostname>.local so you can reach it at
            http://esp32-sofa.local/ instead of hunting for the DHCP address.
            macOS resolves .local hostnames natively.

            The mDNS component is a managed dependency from the Espressif
            Component Registry, so the first build needs network access.

    config SOFA_MDNS_HOSTNAME
        string "mDNS hostname"
        default "esp32-sofa"
        depends on SOFA_ENABLE_MDNS
        help
            The board answers to "<this>.local". Lowercase letters, digits and
            hyphens only, and it must not collide with another device on the
            LAN - change it if you flash a second board.

endmenu
